LeetCode 106 — Construct Binary Tree from Inorder and Postorder Traversal
1. Problem Understanding
Given two arrays, inorder and postorder, construct and return the original binary tree.
- Inorder: Left → Root → Right
- Postorder: Left → Right → Root
Example:
inorder   = [9, 3, 15, 20, 7]
postorder = [9, 15, 7, 20, 3]


Expected tree:
             3
            / \
           9   20
              /  \
             15   7


The critical observation is:

Postorder tells us the root; inorder tells us how to divide the left and right subtrees.

In postorder, the last element is always the root of the current subtree:
postorder = [9, 15, 7, 20, 3]
                                   ↑
                                 Root = 3


Find 3 in inorder:
inorder = [9 | 3 | 15, 20, 7]
           Left Root      Right


So:
Left subtree  = [9]
Right subtree = [15, 20, 7]


We repeat this process recursively until every node has been created.

Approach 1 — Brute Force: Recursion with Array Slicing
Idea
For every recursive call:
1. Take the last element of postorder as the root.
2. Find that root in inorder using linear search.
3. Divide inorder into left and right subtree arrays.
4. Divide postorder into matching subtree arrays.
5. Recursively build both subtrees.
This is the simplest approach to understand, but it repeatedly searches and copies arrays.
Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] inorder, int[] postorder) {

        if (inorder.length == 0) {
            return null;
        }

        int n = inorder.length;

        // Last element in postorder is the root.
        int rootValue = postorder[n - 1];
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(rootValue);

        // Find root in inorder.
        int rootIndex = 0;

        while (inorder[rootIndex] != rootValue) {
            rootIndex++;
        }

        // Number of nodes in the left subtree.
        int leftSize = rootIndex;

        int[] leftInorder =
                Arrays.copyOfRange(inorder, 0, rootIndex);

        int[] rightInorder =
                Arrays.copyOfRange(inorder, rootIndex + 1, n);

        // Exclude the last postorder element because it is the root.
        int[] leftPostorder =
                Arrays.copyOfRange(postorder, 0, leftSize);

        int[] rightPostorder =
                Arrays.copyOfRange(postorder, leftSize, n - 1);

        root.left = buildTree(leftInorder, leftPostorder);
        root.right = buildTree(rightInorder, rightPostorder);

        return root;
    }
}


