Complexity
- Time: \(O(n^2)\) in the worst case because of repeated searches and array copying.
- Auxiliary space: \(O(n^2)\) in the worst case because recursive calls can retain copied arrays.

Approach 2 — Recursive Index Ranges + Linear Search
Instead of creating new arrays at every call, we can pass index ranges into the original arrays.

Your supplied code uses this approach.
Idea
Each recursive call receives four boundaries:
inStart, inEnd
postStart, postEnd


The root is:
int rootVal = postorder[postEnd];


Find this root in the current inorder range.

Then calculate:
leftSize  = rootIndex - inStart
rightSize = inEnd - rootIndex


The left subtree's postorder segment contains leftSize elements. The right subtree's segment contains rightSize elements immediately before the root.
Your code
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode() {}
 *     TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */

class Solution {

    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] inorder, int[] postorder) {
        return buildNode(
                inorder, 0, inorder.length - 1,
                postorder, 0, postorder.length - 1
        );
    }

    public TreeNode buildNode(
            int inorder[],
            int inStart,
            int inEnd,
            int postorder[],
            int postStart,
            int postEnd) {

        if (inStart > inEnd || postStart > postEnd) {
            return null;
        }

        int rootVal = postorder[postEnd];
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(rootVal);

        int rootIndex = 0;

        for (int i = inStart; i <= inEnd; i++) {
            if (inorder[i] == rootVal) {
                rootIndex = i;
                break;
            }
        }

        int leftSize = rootIndex - inStart;
        int rightSize = inEnd - rootIndex;

        root.left = buildNode(
                inorder,
                inStart,
                rootIndex - 1,
                postorder,
                postStart,
                postStart + leftSize - 1
        );

        root.right = buildNode(
                inorder,
                rootIndex + 1,
                inEnd,
                postorder,
                postEnd - rightSize,
                postEnd - 1
        );

        return root;
    }
}


This works for valid input with unique node values, as guaranteed by LeetCode 106.
